            <ArticleTitle>Anthropometric, functional capacity, and oxidative stress changes in Brazilian community-living elderly subjects. A longitudinal study.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>140-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.archger.2016.05.013</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0167-4943(16)30099-1</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To examine the changes and relationships among anthropometric, functional and plasma oxidative stress markers in elderly.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">longitudinal study.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">measurements in 2008 and 2010.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PARTICIPANTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">103 community-dwelling men and women aged 67-92.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MEASUREMENTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Anthropometric parameters [waist, hip, arm and calf circumferences; waist-hip ratio, triceps skinfold thickness and others], basic (ADL) and instrumental activities of daily living (IADL)] and plasma oxidative stress markers (α-tocopherol, β-carotene and malondialdehyde) were assessed in 2008 and 2010.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">ADL, IADL, body weight, skinfold thickness and circumferences of calf and arm decreased and waist and waist-hip ratio increased from 2008 to 2010. α-Tocopherol decreased and malondialdehyde plasma levels increased during the study period. In multiple logistic regression analyses, increased age (OR=1.12; IC: 1.02-1.23; p=0.02), female gender (OR=8.43; IC: 1.23-57.58; p=0.03), hypertension (OR=0.22; IC: 0.06-0.79; p=0.02), arthritis/arthrosis (OR=0.09; IC: 0.009-0.87; p=0.04) and depression (OR=0.20; IC: 0.04-1.03; p=0.05) were independent risk factors for functional decline.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Fat reduction, muscle loss, central obesity increase, functional decline and worsening of plasma oxidative stress were observed during 2-year follow-up. Some of the risk factors that were identified could be modified to help prevent functional decline in elderly. The factors deserving attention include hypertension, arthritis/arthrosis and depression.</AbstractText>
